The key issue in `load_protocols_file_fd`:

```c
while(1) {
    char *line = buffer;
    int line_len = buffer_len;

    while(((line = fgets(line, line_len, fd)) != NULL)
          && strlen(line) > 0
          && (line[strlen(line) - 1] != '\n')) {
      i = strlen(line);
      old_buffer = buffer;
      old_buffer_len = buffer_len;
      buffer_len += chunk_len;

      buffer = ndpi_realloc(old_buffer, old_buffer_len, buffer_len);
      ...
      line = &buffer[i];
      line_len = chunk_len;
    } /* while */

    if(!line) /* safety check */
      break;

    i = strlen(buffer);
    ...
}
```

The bug: When `fgets` reads a line that fills the buffer completely (i.e., reads `line_len - 1` characters without a newline), it reallocs. But there's a subtle issue with the `fgets` behavior.

Actually, let me think about this more carefully. `fgets(line, line_len, fd)` reads at most `line_len - 1` characters. If the line is exactly `line_len - 1` characters long (no newline), then `fgets` returns non-NULL, `strlen(line) = line_len - 1 > 0`, and `line[line_len - 2] != '\n'`, so we realloc and continue.

But wait - there's a critical bug. When `fgets` reads exactly `line_len - 1` characters, the last character read is at `line[line_len - 2]`, and `line[line_len - 1] = '\0'`. So `strlen(line) = line_len - 1`. Then `line[strlen(line) - 1] = line[line_len - 2]`. If this is not `\n`, we realloc.

After realloc, `line = &buffer[i]` where `i = strlen(line) = line_len - 1`. So the new `fgets` reads into `buffer[line_len - 1]` with `line_len = chunk_len = 1024`. This reads up to 1023 more chars. That seems fine.

